welcome back to serverless migration  station where we show google cloud users  one specific migration they can take to  modernize their apps  this is the video for module one  focusing on helping app engine users  switch from web app to flask a popular  web framework in the python community  doing so opens the door to additional  modernization techniques  hi i'm wesley from google here to lead  your journey joined by my colleague  martin  and our little friend porter who will  take you from point a to point b  he was happy to help but why are we  focusing  on web app too i thought app engine  supported many  web frameworks yes that is absolutely  true martin while users  can use other frameworks with app engine  web app 2 comes bundled with  app engine making it the default choice  for many developers and unfortunately  not supported in python 3.  by the way if you've been using a  framework that's not web app you may  actually be in better shape  app engine is now a more flexible  platform so moving away from bundled  services like web app 2 will make users  apps more portable  also flask has more users it has more  resources and is better supported  now flask isn't the only option and  generally any whiskey compliant  framework that does routing is supported  ok so i get it for the web framework uh  but what about all those app engine  bundled service apis well have you heard  of cloud datastore and cloud  tasks well they started out as app  engine datastore and task queues  and since then they've matured to  becoming their own standalone cloud  products where you'll find continuing  new features and innovation  we'll tackle those in other migration  modules but this one focuses just on  flask  all right you said we're going to  migrate a sample app  to flask uh what app well in the module  zero video we gave an overview of the  baseline app we're starting from  so check it out if you missed it okay  i'd like to  follow what you're doing to the code on  my own computer  can you point me to the source code sure  thing martin here's the link  and by the way the repo is maintained  regularly unlike videos  so the code may be newer there let's  pause so all of you can go clone the  repo or download the zip file  got it great let's go to the computer  now and start migrating that module zero  code and turn it into the module one  code  all right let's start from a good place  grab the module zero code from the repo  and deploy to app engine  if you've been around you're probably  expecting to do an app config update  there's a newer and more powerful cli  called gcloud that comes with a cloud  sdk  it gives you access to most cloud tools  instead of just one  so to upload to app engine use gcloud  app deploy  what doesn't change is that your app  should be deployed and available  around the world in less than a minute  starting with the application file  main.py swap the flask imports for web  app 2s  next while web app 2 is initialized at  the bottom with its routing table  you can do it at the top with flask your  app is more complex but these basic  changes apply regardless  next this app only has one route which  goes to main handler  since we already know this you can wipe  the table but when migrating your app  don't delete it quite yet  you'll probably have many more routes  which you need to track so just comment  it out for now once you get to this  point  there's no change to the way we access  datastore the data model and  accompanying rewrite calls stay as is  the handler represents the majority of  the affected code  web app 2 uses the handler classes and  corresponding http methods  in flask a handler is just a route  decorated function like this  finally both frameworks render the  template in a similar way so you just  switch to how flask does it  with that you're done mining grading the  main application  for this migration app.yaml stays the  same  the index.html template file is  identical to the original with one minor  but notable difference  the django style templates that web app  2 uses automatically executes callables  whereas the jinja 2 templating system  that flask uses by default  requires users to explicitly add  parentheses  the datetime c time call turns  timestamps into human readable strings  and that's used in this app to show when  visits occurred  flask also organizes web templates in a  templates folder by default  so create it and move index.html into it  before or after your edit to the c  time column since flask isn't bundled  like web app 2  you must add it to a list of required  packages the baseline app didn't have  any  so create a brand new requirements.txt  file with just flask  now if you're unfamiliar with the  requirements.txt file check out its  documentation  for us the current version is one one  two for a simple app you probably don't  need the version  but in any case the repo is maintained  while this video isn't  meaning you may see a newer version  there  first gen app engine runtimes require  developers to explicitly bundle third  party  for our app it's flask and its  dependencies  so run this pip install command to  install them to the live folder before  you test or deploy  a side effect of bundling third-party  packages is that you need to tell app  engine where to find them  so that's what app engine config dot py  is for  to tell the system to look in lib for  your apps you'll create this file in the  same folder as your application code  just like what we've done with this  sample  the only other files in the repo are  readme and gcloud ignore  neither of which are critical but gcloud  ignore is useful in preventing  unnecessary files to be uploaded and  that's it  hey wes thanks for showing us how to  move to flask watching this video is  great but is there a way for me to do  that migration by hand on my own time  on that sample app before migrating my  own code  well i'm glad you asked martin yes in  addition to this video we have a  corresponding google code lab codelabs  are free online self-paced hands-on  tutorials  so you can do this same migration on  your own time  the repo links you to the code lab the  starting module zero code  as well as the finished module one code  if you get stuck you can always go back  to module 0 or compare your solution to  module 1  to see where you may have gotten off  course for your convenience  here are the docs to both frameworks so  that you can compare and contrast and  better understand the differences  between them  now that you've shown us how to unlink  part of our app from dedicated app  engine libraries uh like  web app two framework uh what's next was  well the module zero app used both  web app 2 and the ndb library to talk to  datastore  now that we're done with the framework  part we're going to be focused on moving  from  ndb to cloud ndb so be sure to join us  for that  this is wesley from google cloud on  behalf of martin and porter  thanks for tuning in today and we look  forward to seeing you at the next  serverless migration station as well as  on other serverless expeditions

